<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Algorithms, Model, and Datasets</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments are conducted on two models <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-4B</span> and<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-4B" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-4B</a></span></span></span> <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qwen3-8B</span> <span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-8B" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-8B</a></span></span></span> with three math reasoning tasks. We use a diverse set of mathematical reasoning datasets for training, including <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DAPO-Math-17k<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">4</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/open-r1/DAPO-Math-17k-Processed" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/datasets/open-r1/DAPO-Math-17k-Processed</a></span></span></span></span>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepMath-103K<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">5</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/zwhe99/DeepMath-103K" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/datasets/zwhe99/DeepMath-103K</a></span></span></span></span>, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">DeepScaleR<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">6</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                  
                <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/agentica-org/DeepScaleR-Preview-Dataset" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_medium">https://huggingface.co/datasets/agentica-org/DeepScaleR-Preview-Dataset</a></span></span></span></span>.
To evaluate the final performance, we use the <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AIME-2024</span> benchmark<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <a href="http://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceH4/aime_2024" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">http://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceH4/aime_2024</a></span></span></span>, a collection of recent challenging math reasoning problems. This benchmark serves as a standard for assessing the advanced mathematical reasoning abilities of large language models.
To faithfully validate the robustness of the proposed method, we apply APRIL to the two most widely used RL algorithms: GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mroueh et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and DAPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hardware Platform</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">APRIL can operate effectively across diverse hardware platforms. We primarily develop and evaluate it on a single node equipped with either 8× NVIDIA H100 GPUs or 8× AMD MI300 GPUs. Due to page limitations, the experimental results presented in the following sections focus mainly on the AMD 8× MI300 configuration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hyperparameter Setting</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ APRIL: Active Partial Rollouts in Reinforcement Learning to tame long-tail generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we summarize the hyperparameters used in our experiments. In our setup, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rollout_batch_size=32</span> specifies the number of input instances, which corresponds to the batch size for a single training step. With <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">n_samples_per_prompt=8</span>, each input instance generates multiple rollouts, resulting in a total of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="32\times 8=256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">32\times 8=256</annotation></semantics></math> samples collected per step. For APRIL, as mentioned earlier, we over-provision rollout requests by setting <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">over_sampling_batch_size=64</span> (<span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">2<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>rollout_batch_size</span>), meaning that 512 samples are requested from the inference engine, but the rollout process terminates once the first 256 rollouts are completed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>The Long-Tail Problem in Rollout</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the long-tail problem, we first conducted a pure rollout experiment without model training. In our analysis, we selected representative datasets and generated four rollouts for each input instance, averaging their lengths per instance. A maximum generation length of 32,768 tokens was imposed to fully capture the tail behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure  3 ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ APRIL: Active Partial Rollouts in Reinforcement Learning to tame long-tail generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the distribution indicates that more than half of the rollout responses terminate within a few thousand tokens, while a long tail of outliers extends close to the maximum limit. This skewness implies that, in a standard synchronous rollout, the completion time of a batch is dominated by the slowest-generating samples, resulting in substantial GPU idle time, as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure  1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ APRIL: Active Partial Rollouts in Reinforcement Learning to tame long-tail generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center">
